<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercenary War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/241-bce">241</a>–<a href="/years/238-bce">238 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">Mercenary</a> War was a revolt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s unpaid mercenary army and its Libyan subjects from 241 to 238 BCE, following the First Punic War. The rebellion threatened Carthage itself before it was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> used the crisis to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, weakening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercenary-war.jpg" alt="Mercenary War" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Libyan_Revolt,_shekel,_241-238_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> ended with the Treaty of Lutatius, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> evacuated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and paid an indemnity of 3,200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. Carthaginian armies were nearly always composed of foreigners, most of them North Africans, whom Roman sources call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The 20,000 soldiers of the army in Sicily were sent back to Carthage in small detachments by Gisco, in the expectation that they would be paid their years of back pay. The authorities instead hoped to settle at a lower rate. When the Senate refused to pay in full the men marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Gisco brought money, but discipline broke down, Gisco was taken prisoner, and Spendius and Matho were declared generals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>African towns resentful of Carthaginian taxation rose in revolt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says 70,000 men joined the rebels. Hanno won the Battle of Utica in early 240 BCE but lost the counter-attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, given command of a smaller force, defeated the rebels at the Battle of the Bagradas River, and the Numidian leader Naravas then changed sides with 2,000 cavalry.</p><p>Hamilcar treated captured rebels leniently. Spendius and the Gaul Autaritus had 700 Carthaginian prisoners, Gisco among them, tortured to death, and from then on neither side gave mercy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> called it the Truceless War. Utica and Hippo joined the rebels, who besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> until supplies failed. Hamilcar trapped Spendius's army in a pass called the Saw, where it was massacred, and the leaders he had taken were crucified. Hanno and Hamilcar together later destroyed the remaining 30,000 rebels, and Matho was tortured to death in Carthage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had declined an appeal from the mutinous garrisons on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, but when the islanders drove them out, probably in 237 BCE, it agreed to intervene. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> prepared its own expedition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> called this an act of war and required the cession of Sardinia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and an extra indemnity of 1,200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> considered this bad faith the main cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Hamilcar's victory raised the standing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a>, and Carthage's generals were afterwards chosen by their armies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mercenary War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mercenary War</b> of 241–238 BCE showed as much, when unpaid veterans of the First Punic War turned on Carthage itself in a brutal conflict that Gustave Flaubert later fictionalised in Salammbô.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 238 BCE, after its defeat in the First Punic War, Carthage surrendered Sardinia to Rome during the <b>Mercenary War</b>, and with Corsica the island became the Roman province of Corsica et Sardinia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It became an ally of Carthage and part of the Punic Empire but often kept its distance, joining the rebels in the <b>Mercenary War</b> of 240 to 238 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/utica">Utica</a></li></ul></section>
